.TH CHPC-SEC-VULN-HELPER 1 "2026-09-29" "CHPC_sec_vuln_helper 0.1.0" "Coastal HPC"
.SH NAME
chpc-sec-vuln-helper \- audit RHEL/Rocky/Windows hosts for security weaknesses on an airgapped machine and produce a branded report
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B python3 -m chpc_sec_vuln_helper
.I command
.RI [ arguments ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B chpc-sec-vuln-helper
audits RHEL/Rocky 8, 9, 10 and Windows 10, 11 hosts for security
weaknesses, and writes a branded, plain-English report anyone can act
on. Two independent kinds of findings: baseline/hardening checks of
real settings (self-contained, never goes stale), and CVE matches
against an official offline vulnerability feed you import
periodically (Red Hat OVAL for RHEL/Rocky, an MSRC CSV export for
Windows). Fixing what it finds is left entirely to you; nothing this
tool does changes anything on the scanned machine.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI collect " [-o FILE]"
Collect this Linux host's security-relevant state into JSON (default:
stdout).
.I collect
only works on Linux; on Windows, run
.I collectors/windows_collector.ps1
directly instead, with the same JSON output shape.
.TP
.BI update-feed " [options]"
Fetch or import a vulnerability feed.
.RS
.TP
.BI --os-family " rhel|rocky"
With
.BR --os-version ,
check for and offer to fetch/import an update for this OS family
(the "roster update" prompt).
.TP
.BI --os-version " N"
Major version number, e.g. 9.
.TP
.BI --import-rhel-oval " FILE"
Normalize and import an already-downloaded RHEL OVAL
.I .xml
or
.I .xml.bz2
file. Requires
.BR --os-version .
Also populates the matching Rocky Linux feed (see README for why).
.TP
.BI --import-msrc-csv " FILE"
Normalize and import a manually-exported MSRC Security Update Guide
CSV file.
.TP
.BI --feed-dir " DIR"
Where feeds are stored (default:
.IR chpc_sec_vuln_helper/feed_data/ ).
.RE
.TP
.BI list-feeds " [--feed-dir DIR]"
Show every locally stored feed, its advisory count, and its age.
.TP
.BI scan " COLLECTED_JSON [options]"
Evaluate a collected JSON file against the baseline rules and any
loaded CVE feed, and write the report.
.RS
.TP
.BI --out " PATH"
Output path without extension (default:
.IR security_report ).
.TP
.BI --format " docx|html|both"
Which report format(s) to write (default: both). Falls back to
.I html
only if
.B python-docx
isn't installed.
.TP
.B --no-feed-prompt
Never show the "is your feed current?" prompt.
.TP
.B --yes
Answer "no" to the feed-update prompt automatically (non-interactive;
uses whatever feed is already loaded).
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo python3 -m chpc_sec_vuln_helper collect -o collected.json
python3 -m chpc_sec_vuln_helper update-feed --os-family rhel --os-version 9
python3 -m chpc_sec_vuln_helper scan collected.json --out report --format both
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I chpc_sec_vuln_helper/feed_data/
Default location of locally stored, normalized vulnerability feeds
(one file per OS family/major version) and the
.I incoming/
drop folder for manually-exported MSRC CSVs awaiting import.
.TP
.I chpc_sec_vuln_helper/rules/linux_rules.py
.TQ
.I chpc_sec_vuln_helper/rules/windows_rules.py
The baseline/hardening rule definitions.
.TP
.I chpc_sec_vuln_helper/collectors/
The Linux (Python) and Windows (PowerShell) collectors.
.SH A NOTE ON HONESTY
This tool cannot promise to find "all" vulnerabilities \-\- no offline
tool can, without a live, constantly-updated CVE database, which is
exactly what an airgapped machine doesn't have. Baseline checks are
always accurate (they check real settings, not a database); CVE
matching is exactly as current as the feed last imported, and the
report always states that date plainly.
.SH SEE ALSO
This package's own
.IR README.md ,
shipped alongside this tool, covers the full workflow, requirements,
and why Rocky Linux reuses the RHEL feed.
.SH AUTHOR
Coastal HPC.
